pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% table(x1, x2) in {(0,0), (1,1)}.  Once x1 = 0, the only tuple that could
% support x2 = 1 is (1,1), and that tuple needs x1 = 1.  So x2 = 1 goes.
%
% Unlike the linear case this one IS reverse unit propagation, and the reason
% is the encoding: each tuple has a selector atom, the selectors are pinned by
% an at-least-one constraint, and asserting x1eq0 falsifies every selector in
% turn.  Nothing here needs cutting planes.
rup x1eq0 ==> 1 ~x2eq1 >= 1 ;
output NONE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
